(function(payload){if(typeof applySerialChapterPayload==="function")applySerialChapterPayload(payload);else (window.SERIAL_CHAPTER_QUEUE||(window.SERIAL_CHAPTER_QUEUE=[])).push(payload);})({"id":"serial-cultivation-08","book":"mountain-ledger","cn":["天亮前，庙后传来拖拽声和沉重的[[footstep|脚步声]]。周伯与顾峦被人拿顾衡的印令接走，说一个去看孙女，一个回山治热病。草席破了，末页也不见了。顾衡赶回时，怀里多了韩长老的信：[[provided|只要]]交出银扣，他便能入门陪弟弟。入夜，沈砚仍赴许庭的栈房，想把那车粮拿稳。掌柜声称这买卖是[[mutual|相互的]]帮忙，却问他肯不肯用旧账换粮。沈砚没端茶，先请顾衡说清，他打算怎样[[respond|回应]]长老的条件。","顾衡当着两人的面[[acknowledge|承认]]，自己曾私下写信向长老求药，以为家事可以不写入契纸。罗清把私信和回函收好，让他写出一份[[statement|声明]]。庙里有人骂他[[traitor|叛徒]]，也有人说换了自己未必做得到。沈砚没替他求原谅，只问还能做什么。顾衡愿带扣赴约，靠能查验的行动做出[[amends|弥补]]。许庭看见两封信，也承认断粮并非天灾，是门中有人授意。他写下供粮数量，仍不肯说是谁，沈砚让他先把答应的粮送走。","沈砚没有独自拿扣冲山。他提出[[prudent|谨慎的]]交换：罗清保管全部账，自己通知两村到钟楼下，顾衡先验人，再谈交扣。要[[rescue|营救]]家人，不能使别的病人又断药断水。周伯可能为孙女改口，他们须[[anticipate|预料]]这种变化，药样、旧账与水量必须彼此对应，留下相互[[consistent|一致的]]记录，不能只靠一句供词。沈砚把条件写成三份，连许庭都留了一份。窗外马蹄声逼近，店主叫他们快走，他却先等粮车驶出院门。","夜里的山阶上，韩长老身旁摆着盖布的担架，称再拖便会害死顾峦。顾衡几乎伸手交扣，却听见布下的咳声太响——弟弟肿着喉咙，根本发不出这样的声音。他要求当面[[identify|确认身份]]，掀开布角，见到的竟是药铺掌柜。韩长老并不慌，只说救人的故事最容易[[deceive|欺骗]]救过人的年轻人。顾衡看清了：被[[manipulate|操纵]]的是他怕弟弟来不及救治的[[instinct|本能]]。他将担架推离门口，退到众人能看见的地方。","长老抬手要[[seize|夺取]]银扣，顾衡后退一步，吹响工人的哨子。钟楼下灯火接连亮起，罗清举着药样，沈砚要求公开放人。许庭的粮车也停在山阶外，掌柜这次没敢替门中喊话。韩长老不能当众抢扣，改提最后一笔[[bargain|交易]]：交出账末一页，便放周伯、顾峦，并归还铜匙。顾衡把扣子还给沈砚，带着[[shame|羞愧]]站在旁边。那份信任还未[[restore|恢复]]，但今夜要交换什么，终于不再只有门内一人说了算。"],"en":["Before dawn, dragging sounds and heavy [[footstep|footsteps]] came from behind the temple. Men carrying an order bearing Gu's seal had taken Zhou and Gu Luan away, claiming one would see his granddaughter and the other receive treatment for fever. The mat was torn, and the final page was gone. Gu returned with Han's letter: [[provided|provided]] he surrendered the clasp, he could visit his brother inside the mountain. That night Shen still went to Xu's storehouse to secure the promised grain. The merchant called the deal [[mutual|mutual]] assistance but asked for the old ledger in exchange. Shen left his tea untouched and first asked how Gu intended to [[respond|respond]] to Han's terms.","Before the others, Gu [[acknowledge|acknowledged]] that he had privately requested medicine from Han, thinking family business could stay outside their agreement. Luo kept both letters and asked him to write a [[statement|statement]]. At the temple, some called him a [[traitor|traitor]]; others doubted they could have done better. Shen did not ask anyone to forgive him. He asked what could still be done. Gu would carry the clasp to the meeting and make [[amends|amends]] through verifiable action. Seeing the letters, Xu admitted the grain shortage had been ordered by someone in the sect. He wrote down his promised quantity without naming the person. Shen told him to send the grain first.","Shen would not rush uphill alone with the clasp. He proposed a [[prudent|prudent]] exchange. Luo would keep all the accounts, Shen would summon both villages below the tower, and Gu would see the patients before discussing the clasp. Acting to [[rescue|rescue]] relatives must not deprive other patients of medicine or water. They had to [[anticipate|anticipate]] that Zhou might change his story for his granddaughter. The samples, ledgers and water figures must remain [[consistent|consistent]] with one another, rather than resting on one statement. Shen wrote three copies of the terms, leaving one even with Xu. Hooves approached outside. The merchant urged them to leave, but Shen first waited until the grain cart left the yard.","On the mountain steps that night, Han had a covered stretcher beside him. He claimed further delay would kill Gu Luan. Gu nearly handed over the clasp, then heard a cough too strong for his brother's swollen throat. He insisted on uncovering the patient to [[identify|identify]] him. Beneath the cloth was the medicine shopkeeper. Han remained calm. Stories of rescue, he said, could easily [[deceive|deceive]] young men who had rescued others. Gu understood the [[instinct|instinct]] Han hoped to [[manipulate|manipulate]]: his fear that treatment would come too late for his brother. He pushed the stretcher away from the doorway and stepped back where everyone could see him.","Han reached to [[seize|seize]] the clasp. Gu stepped back and blew a worker's whistle. Lamps lit one after another below the tower. Luo raised the medicine samples, and Shen demanded the patients' public release. Xu's grain cart had stopped beyond the steps; this time its owner did not speak for the sect. Unable to take the clasp openly, Han offered a final [[bargain|bargain]]: the last ledger page in exchange for Zhou, Gu Luan and the copper key. Gu returned the clasp to Shen and stood beside him in [[shame|shame]]. They had not managed to [[restore|restore]] trust, but that night one man behind a door no longer dictated every exchange."]});
